using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace TransitTimetables
{
    // Machine-translated UI strings (Options + panels). English is the source of truth; Get() returns the
    // locale string when present, else English. Community corrections welcome.
    public static class Translations
    {
        public static string Get(string key, string locale) =>
            (Loc.TryGetValue(locale, out var lm) && lm.TryGetValue(key, out var lv)) ? lv
            : (En.TryGetValue(key, out var v) ? v : key);

        public static readonly Dictionary<string, string> En = new Dictionary<string, string>
        {
            { "opt.ManageVehicleCount.D", "When on (default), the mod sets each managed line's vehicle count to the number you chose for the current time of day. Turn it off to leave vehicle counts to you or another fleet mod (like All Transit + Truck) — the mod still evens the line out at its terminus, it just won't add or remove vehicles. A line with fewer vehicles then simply runs a wider interval." },
            { "opt.ManageVehicleCount.L", "Manage vehicle count" },
            { "opt.ProvisionRealFleet.D", "Also size each line's vehicle count to its REAL loop instead of the estimate. This is the one that spends: holding a tight headway on a line whose real loop is ~2x the estimate needs about twice the vehicles, and twice the upkeep. OFF (default) keeps the estimate-based count, so a line silently runs a longer effective headway than you set; ON provisions for the real loop. Grow-only — it never cuts a line below the estimate — and capped, so a bad reading can't flood you with buses." },
            { "opt.ProvisionRealFleet.L", "Provision fleet for real travel time (increases number of vehicles)" },
            { "opt.RealisticTravelTime.D", "The game's own estimate of how long a line takes undershoots the real, simulated loop — buses accelerate and brake at every stop, which the estimate ignores (measured live at roughly 1.7x on sparse lines up to ~2.5x on stop-dense ones). Turn this ON and the mod measures each line's real loop and posts realistic stop times, so the board matches what the buses actually do. Costs nothing — it only makes the clock honest. OFF by default; existing timetables are unchanged until you enable it." },
            { "opt.RealisticTravelTime.L", "Realistic travel time (correct posted times)" },
            { "opt.CleanUninstall.L", "Remove all mod data from this save" },
            { "opt.CleanUninstall.W", "This will DELETE every line's service plan in this city and return all lines to the base game's behaviour. Your per-line vehicle counts, custom peak windows and measured travel data will be lost and cannot be recovered.\n\nIMPORTANT: after confirming, close this menu and let the game RUN for a moment, then save. The cleanup happens on the next simulation step, so saving while paused would save your city unchanged.\n\nContinue?" },
            { "opt.CleanUninstall.D", "One-time cleanup for a clean uninstall. Reverts every line to vanilla (releases waiting vehicles, restores unbunching, drops the mod-set vehicle count) and removes the mod's saved data from this city (your per-line vehicle counts, custom peaks and measured travel). Press this, then let the game run for a moment (the cleanup happens on the next simulation step, so it does nothing while paused), then SAVE — after that the mod can be removed with no leftover residue. It clears your service plans, so it asks for confirmation first. Harmless if you press it with the mod still installed — the lines just go back to plain vanilla." },
            { "opt.MaxDwellRoad.L", "Maximum stop time — buses (minutes)" },
            { "opt.MaxDwellRoad.D", "How long a bus may keep waiting, once it is due to leave, for passengers who are ALREADY boarding. It is a limit, not a target: a bus with nobody boarding leaves the moment it is due, and this setting never delays it by a second. At an ordinary stop 'due' means as soon as it arrived, which is what makes an ordinary stop take exactly as long as the boarding does. It exists because the base game counts someone as a passenger the moment they start walking to the vehicle, so departing hard would push everyone still crossing the platform back into the queue. Default 3 minutes. Set it to 0 to wait for nobody. Note: this works by shortening the base game's own boarding window, so it can never make a vehicle wait LONGER than the game would." },
            { "opt.MaxDwellRail.L", "Maximum stop time — trains, metros and trams (minutes)" },
            { "opt.MaxDwellRail.D", "The same limit for rail, also 3 minutes by default. Raise it if you see people piling up on a busy platform: a train or metro exchanging a full platform takes far longer to load than a bus, its passengers spread out along the platform to reach their own carriage, and unlike a bus it can never skip a stop. Bear in mind the allowance applies at every stop, so a generous value on a long line adds up around the loop — and a longer round trip means the same number of vehicles produces a wider interval." },
            { "opt.StopAtEveryStop.L", "Stop at every stop (even empty ones)" },
            { "opt.StopAtEveryStop.D", "By default Cities: Skylines II lets a bus SKIP a stop where nobody is waiting and nobody wants to get off — it only slows and rolls through. That matters most at a terminus: a vehicle that rolls past it never starts boarding, so the mod never gets to space it out. Both timing points (the terminus and Terminus B) are therefore always forced to stop, regardless of this setting. Turn this ON to force vehicles to pull in at EVERY stop on a managed line — at the cost of a short dwell at each empty one, which lengthens the round trip and so widens the interval a little. Affects buses and other road vehicles only; trains, trams, ships and aircraft already stop at every station. OFF by default." },
            { "opt.RealisticTripsCompat.L", "Realistic Trips / slow-time compatibility — turn ON if you run RT or Time2Work (ALPHA)" },
            { "opt.RealisticTripsCompat.D", "OFF by default — leave it off unless you run a mod that lengthens the in-game day (Time2Work / \\\"Realistic Trips\\\" or similar). Turn it ON with such a mod and departures, stop times and fleet sizes stay correct instead of running early; the mod measures the real day length automatically. With no slow-time mod this setting does nothing, and if the mod detects one while this is OFF it will note it in the log. NOTE: this compatibility path is an ALPHA and has NOT been tested against Realistic Trips in-game yet — please report anything odd. Under Realistic Trips your lines will correctly use fewer vehicles than before." },
